  "query_preview": "through Saturday evening. \n\nAn east/west band of showers and storms has developed in the \nvicinity of either the stationary boundary or a residual outflow \nboundary from convection earlier in the morning. Despite roughly \n1500-2000 J/kg of MLCAPE, poor shear should prevent adequate \norganization and severe potential. CAMs continue to struggle, but \nsome more recent runs suggest we could see this band of showers and \nembedded storms continue through parts of the evening before \ndissipating. \n\nNot too dissimilar from previous nights, scattered storm development \nis expected to kick off over the Central/High Plains, growing \nupscale over time leading to an MCS rolling through much of Nebraska \nand Iowa. Depending on the southern extent of this development, we \ncould once again see storms crossing into northern Kansas and \nMissouri Thursday morning. Currently have 15-30% PoPs in our \nnorthern fringes tomorrow morning but wouldn't be surprised if these \nneed to be increased in later forecast cycles. If storms are able to \ntap into a locally conducive environment, we may be able to see some \nsmall hail and damaging wind gusts. Unfortunately, the most likely \narea for any severe weather to occur would be across northern and \nnortheastern Missouri, an area that has faced the brunt of severe \nweather these last few days. SPC has a Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 5) \nfor severe weather in north/northeastern Missouri tonight into \ntomorrow morning. \n\nShower and storm chances do not end tomorrow morning, however, an \nadditional shortwave is expected to take advantage of the nocturnal \nlow-level jet tomorrow evening into Friday morning, once again \nrenewing storm chances late Thursday night into Friday morning and \npotentially into the afternoon hours, though that is much lower \nconfidence at this time. If storms continue into the afternoon or \nredevelopment occurs, we could see overnight storms once again as \nthe low-level jet ramps up. SPC has issued a Marginal Risk for both \nThursday and Friday encompassing the potential for damaging winds \nand hail. Generally, PoPs are between 15-30% over this timeframe, \npeaking during the overnight and early morning hours but bottoming \nout in the afternoon due to uncertainty. \n\nA more widespread \"cooldown\" looks probable Sunday into Monday with \na cold front expected to move through the region sometime during \nthis timeframe. The exact timing of the frontal passage may result \nin a north/south gradient of temperatures on Sunday with \ntemperatures 10-15 degrees cooler in our far north compared to our \nfar south.",
